Ergonomic workplace injuries
Social & Environmental
Repetitive work task correlation, Progressive pain during shift, Localized tenderness/paresthesias, Positive provocative maneuvers

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-040
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of localized neuropathic pain and functional loss in the dominant hand.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing severe, unrelenting, "electric shock" pain and numbness in their dominant hand, rendering them unable to sleep or perform basic self-care tasks after a heavy shift of manual labor. Urgent assessment was required to rule out acute ischemic events or compartment syndrome, and provide immediate neuropathic pain relief prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hand is completely numb, shooting pain is waking me up in tears, and I can't even hold a cup of water."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious onset of localized musculoskeletal pain directly correlated with their repetitive work tasks (factory assembly line worker). Over the last 48 hours, the pain has worsened progressively, peaking during their shift and initially improving with rest, but tonight the pain is severe and unremitting. The patient complains of severe paresthesias (burning, tingling, and numbness) in the thumb, index, and middle fingers of the right hand due to nerve compression. There is a palpable muscle spasm in the right forearm. The patient describes a clear history of prolonged, static awkward postures and repetitive manual labor. They report decreased grip strength and absolute functional impairment tonight. There is a complete absence of acute macrotrauma.
Functional Status: Independent but acutely incapacitated by severe pain and loss of fine motor function in the dominant hand
Social/Safety Concerns: Occupational injury threatening livelihood, severe sleep disruption, inability to perform basic ADLs

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Distressed, cradling the right arm, visibly wincing when the wrist is moved
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals a normal appearing right hand with no swelling, erythema, or deformity. Capillary refill is brisk (<2 secs) and radial pulse is strong. There is localized tenderness over the volar aspect of the wrist. Neurological exam reveals decreased sensation to light touch in the median nerve distribution (thumb, index, middle finger). Diagnostic tetrad features are starkly present: perfect correlation with repetitive work tasks, progressive pain, localized paresthesias, and positive provocative maneuvers (Phalen's test reproduces severe numbness within 15 seconds; Tinel's sign is strongly positive at the carpal tunnel). Grip strength is objectively reduced (4/5) due to pain.
Environmental Observations: Patient is applying a makeshift ice pack to the wrist.
Medication Reconciliation: Took standard doses of paracetamol and ibuprofen with zero neuropathic pain relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ergonomic workplace injuries (Acute severe Carpal Tunnel Syndrome exacerbation) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Cervical Radiculopathy (C6/C7) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of neck pain, normal neck range of motion, and the strongly positive local provocative tests (Tinel's/Phalen's) perfectly localizing the lesion to the wrist.
2. Acute Arterial Occlusion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hand is warm and well-perfused, with a strong radial pulse and brisk capillary refill, contrasting with a cold, pulseless, pale limb.
3. Scaphoid Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absolute lack of acute trauma/fall, and the lack of focal tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering from an acute, severe exacerbation of an entrapment neuropathy (Carpal Tunnel Syndrome) directly caused by unmitigated ergonomic workplace hazards. The pain is severely neuropathic, causing extreme distress, sleep deprivation, and functional loss of the dominant hand. Deferring assessment would have left the patient in agonizing, unremitting pain throughout the night. The objective findings of positive provocative maneuvers and sensory deficits definitively localize the non-life-threatening but highly morbid injury, completely justifying the urgent after-hours intervention to provide specialized neuropathic analgesia, splinting advice, and a medical certificate.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provision of a short course of targeted neuropathic analgesia (e.g., Amitriptyline 10mg stat or Pregabalin) to allow for sleep tonight. Application of a rigid resting wrist splint (in neutral position) from clinician supplies to immediately decompress the nerve. Provision of a medical certificate for 3 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: By wearing this rigid wrist splint strictly every night while you sleep, you prevent your wrist from bending and crushing the nerve, which allows the inflammation to subside and stops the burning pain from waking you up.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Formal documentation initiated for a workers' compensation claim. Referral to an occupational therapist.
DME/Safety (Optional): Rigid volar wrist splint provided.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for formal nerve conduction study referral and ongoing workers' compensation management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Presentation to the Emergency Department if the hand suddenly becomes cold, pale, or blue, or if the pain becomes completely intractable despite the provided medication and splinting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute burning and numbness will improve with the splint and nerve medication, but long-term resolution requires an ergonomic overhaul of your workstation or potentially minor surgery.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your entire hand suddenly goes completely white and cold to the touch, you must go to the emergency department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The repetitive factory work has caused the tendons in your wrist to swell, crushing the main nerve to your hand. Regular painkillers do not work on crushed nerves. The splint holds the tunnel open, and the new medication specifically targets nerve pain so you can sleep.
Actionable Steps: Wear the splint constantly at night and whenever resting. Do not return to the factory line until you have seen your regular GP and the occupational therapist has reviewed your workstation.

